# Untitled

Indium doped CsPbI3 films for inorganic perovskite solar cells with efficiency exceeding $17\%$
Xiaomei Li1,2, Kaili Wang1, Femi Lgbari1, Chong Dong1, Wenfan Yang1, Chang Ma1, Heng Ma2, Zhao-Kui Wang1 $(\boxtimes)$ , and Liang-Sheng Liao
1 Institute of Functional Nano and Soft Materials (FUNSOM), Jiangsu Key Laboratory for Carbon-Based Functional Materials and Devices,   
Soochow University, Suzhou 215123, China   
2 Henan Province Key Laboratory of Photovoltaic Materials, College of Physics & Materials Science, Henan Normal University, Xinxiang 453007, China
$\circledcirc$ Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2020   
Received: 4 February 2020 / Revised: 23 April 2020 / Accepted: 27 April 2020
## ABSTRACT
In recent years, all-inorganic perovskite materials have set off a research boom owing to features, such as good thermal stability, suitable bandgap, and fascinating optical properties. However, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) and the ambient stability of all-inorganic perovskite solar cells still remain a challenge. Herein, we investigate the effect of the addition of $\mathsf{I n l}_{3}$ into CsPbI3 film on the corresponding device. $\mathsf{I n l}_{3}$ incorporation could retard the crystallization process and control the growth rate of $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ polycrystalline films, yielding a high quality film with large grains and few voids. The increment in electrostatic potential and the reduction of carrier recombination enabled the open-circuit voltage of fabricated perovskite solar cell to be increased from 0.89 to $0.99{\textsf{V}}$ . The champion device delivered a power conversion efficiency of $17.09\%$ , which is higher than $14.36\%$ for the reference device. And the $\left\vert\mathsf{n}\right\vert_{3}$ -included solar cell without any encapsulation retained $77\%$ of its original efficiency after $860\mathsf{h}$ aging at room temperature in ${\sf N}_{2}$ condition.
## KEYWORDS
indium doping, CsPbI3, crystallization retardation, stability
## Introduction
Hybrid lead halide perovskite has become one of the most eye-catching materials in recent years due to its low cost, narrow band gap, easy processability, and high efficiency in both solar cells and light-emitting diodes [1, 2]. Particularly, power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $25.2\%$ for photovoltaic devices has been achieved within a relatively short time of ten years [3–7]. However, the easy decomposition of the hybrid lead halide perovskite material at high temperature limits their potential applications [8–10]. Incidentally, inorganic perovskite materials have been presented as alternatives due to their good thermal stability, suitable band gap, and excellent photoelectric performance [11, 12]. They can be realized by substituting the inorganic cation ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ for the volatile organic cation $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}}^{+}$ $(\mathrm{MA^{+}})$ and $[(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{CH}]^{+}$ $(\mathrm{FA}^{+})$ to yield the all-inorganic perovskites CsPbX3 ( $\mathrm{\DeltaX=I^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ or $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ ) [13–15]. As an example, $\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}$ with a high band gap of $2.3\ \mathrm{eV},$ which can limit the absorption of light, shows good stability [16, 17]. In order to change the bandgap, the component ratio of the $\mathrm{X^{-}}$ site can be adjusted accordingly. Wang et al. reported that a $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ film possesses a bandgap of $1.95\ \mathrm{eV}$ [18]. Similarly, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{IBr}_{2}$ has a bandgap of $2.05\ \mathrm{eV}.$ However, the wide bandgaps gave unsatisfactory PCEs. $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ shows a good visible light absorption by its suitable bandgap of $1.73\ \mathrm{eV}$ [19]. Recently, Wang et al. reported a PCE of $19.03\%$ by utilizing phenyltrimethylammonium chlorine (PTACl) as the surface passivation [20]. However, the perovskite is easily transformed from a black phase (α phase) into a yellow phase (δ phase) due to the defective Goldschmidt tolerance factor. To improve the stability, many approaches, such as interface engineering and doping strategy, have been explored. Wang et al. reported the enhanced phase stability of CsPbI3 film via phenyltrimethylammonium bromide (PTABr) treatment [11]. The cell was exposed to ultraviolet (UV) light for $500\mathrm{~h~}$ without impairing the device efficiency [21]. Zhang et al. showed that an addition of HI additive into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ could facilitate the growth of $\upbeta$ -CsPbI3 and $\upgamma$ -CsPbI3 films with improved phase stability [22]. Furthermore, Hu et al. demonstrated that a partial substituting of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ with $\mathrm{Bi^{+}}$ could retard the degradation of perovskite layer effectively [23]. Some other elements, such as $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ [24, 25] and $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ [26], could also partially replace $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ to enhance the resistance of CsPbI3 films from water and oxygen erosion by keeping their black phase. These findings give a clue that suitable substitution is an effective method to improve the device performance of inorganic perovskite solar cells.
Herein, we incorporate $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ into $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ film to investigate its effect on the electrical structure and the photoelectric property of the perovskite. InI3 is chosen by avoiding the intervention of other new elements. $\operatorname{InI}_{3}$ -involved CsPbI3 perovskite presented a high quality film with large grains and few voids owing to the retarding role of InI3 in controlling the perovskite crystallization. The developed strategy resulted in an increase in PCE from $14.36\%$ to $17.09\%$ because of an obvious increase of open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ from 0.89 to $0.99{\mathrm{V}}.$ The fabricated device without encapsulation also demonstrated improved stability by keeping $77\%$ of its initial PCE after $864\mathrm{h}$ storing in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
$\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{DMAI}$ (dimethylammonium iodine, Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp), cesium iodide $(99.99\%$ CsI, Alfa Aesar Ltd), N, N-Dimethylformamide ( $99.9\%$ DMF, Alfa Aesar Ltd), and Spiro-OMeTAD (1-Material Ltd) were used in this work.
## 2.2  Device fabrication
A device structure of fluorine-doped tin oxide $\mathrm{(FTO)}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ CsPbI3 (InI3:CsPbI3)/Spiro-OMeTAD/ $\mathrm{{MoO}_{3}/A g}$ was adopted. The FTO glass substrate was ultrasonically cleaned with detergent, deionized water, acetone, and ethanol in succession. The FTO substrate was then immersed in an aqueous solution of titanium tetrachloride (TiCl4, $200~\mathrm{mL}$ ) and placed in an oven at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ to form a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer. $0.8\mathrm{M}\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ solution was prepared by dissolving PbI2·xDMAI:CsI (1:1) in DMF. The solution was spin-coated on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer at $3,000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $50\mathrm{{s}}$ to form the absorber layer. Holes transport material (HTM) was prepared by dissolving Sprio-OMeTAD $(90~\mathrm{mg})$ , a sufonyl imide (Li-TFSI, $22.5~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ solution ( $520\mathrm{mg}$ Li-TFSI in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ acetonitrile), and tert-butylpyidine (tBP, $36~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}})$ in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of chlorbenzene (CB) solution, and stirring at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Then HTM was spin-coated in the absorbed layer at $5{,}000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $40~\mathsf{s}$ . Finally, $10\ \mathrm{nm\MoO_{3}}$ and $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ silver electrode were slowly evaporated to complete the device.
## 2.3  Measurement and characterization
The current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics were measured by using a programmable Keithley 2,400 source meter under AM 1.5G illumination with a light intensity of $100\mathrm{\mW/cm^{2}}$ . Atomic force microscopy (AFM) images were obtained on a Veeco Multimode V instrument. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analyses were done by using an ESCALAB250Xi (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA). Electrical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was conducted on an IM6e Electrochemical Workstation (ZAHNER, Germany). Capacitance vs. frequency $\left(C\mathrm{-}F\right)$ was measured by Precision Impedance Analyzer (Agilent 4294A). The absorption spectra were obtained using ultraviolet and visible (UV–Vis) spectrophotometer (PerkinElmer Lambda 750). Surface morphologies were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Quanta 200 FEG, FEI Co.). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were achieved on PANalytica 80 equipment.
## 3 Results and discussion
InI3 was directly introduced into CsPbI3 perovskite precursor. $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}{:}\mathrm{In}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ film was deposited by spin-coating the precursor solution onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer with an annealing at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ . Figure 1(a) shows the core levels of the chemical composition of pure CsPbI3 and CsPbI3:InI3 films as obtained from XPS. The Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ core level in $\mathrm{CsPbI_{3}}{:}\mathrm{InI_{3}}$ films is upshifted by $0.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ relative to the pure $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ film. This is originated from the partial substitution of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ by $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ , proving that $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ can be successfully incorporated into the perovskite crystal lattice [27, 28]. As shown in Figs. 1(b) and 1(c), the binding energies of $\mathrm{Cs}3\mathrm{d}$ and I 3d are also upshifted by 0.10 and $0.15\mathrm{eV,}$ respectively, indicating that the force of interaction between Pb-I and Cs-I are enhanced. This means that the structure of
InI3-involved CsPbI3 is more stable and difficult to decompose. As shown in Fig. 1(d), a peak located at $445.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ associated with the In $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ core level is detected in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}{:}\mathrm{In}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ film, confirming the presence of $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ in the perovskite film.
To further study the effect of $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ doping on the crystallization of CsPbI3 film, top-view SEM images of the pristine and doped perovskite films were obtained. As shown in Figs. 2(a) and 2(b), larger grain size can be seen in the CsPbI3:InI3 film. SEM image at micron level also confirmed the enhanced film quality of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ perovskites with enlarged grain size and reduced pin-holes by $\operatorname{InI}_{3}$ incorporation (see Fig. S4 in the ESM). Superior film quality facilitates interfacial charge transfer and reduces charge accumulation [32]. The statistics charts of grain sizes in the pristine and doped CsPbI3 film (Figs. 2(c) and 2(d)) showed an increase in average grain size from 225 to $325~\mathrm{{nm}}$ The effectiveness of grain size optimization by InI3 doping was further confirmed by AFM (see Fig. S5 in the ESM).
In-depth investigation of the effect of $\operatorname{InI}_{3}$ doping on perovskite crystallization was performed. The XRD patterns of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}{:}\mathrm{In}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ films were further recorded at different stages during the annealing process. As shown in Fig. 3(a), the XRD patterns of the pristine CsPbI3 film showed an intermediate phase at $15.8^{\circ}$ . This peak disappeared when the annealing time was prolonged. In contrast, no intermediate phase was observed firstly for CsPbI3:InI3 film. However, the intermediate phase appeared after $30~\mathsf{s}$ annealing (Fig. 3(b)). The difference of the intermediate phase in two films was ascribed to the incorporation of $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ into the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ perovskite. With increasing the annealing time, a weak peak of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ phase was firstly observed in the pristine film (Fig. 3(c)). After annealing, the poor crystallization was shown in the pristine $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ film (Fig. 3(d)). Therefore, it is evident that InI3 doping can retard CsPbI3 crystallization, yielding high quality films with increased grain size and reduced pin-holes.
Figure 4(a) shows the UV–Vis absorption spectra and photoluminescence (PL) spectra of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{CsPbI_{3}}{:}\mathrm{InI_{3}}$ films. Obviously, the absorption of $\mathrm{InI}_{3}$ -doped CsPbI3 film is enhanced compared with the pristine $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ film. The PL peak $(726~\mathrm{nm})$ ) is red-shifted by $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ relative to the pristine film at $724~\mathrm{nm}$ . This is consistent with the absorption spectra. The slight red-shift and the enhanced absorption are ascribed to the large grain size and good charge transfer capability [29, 33–35]. At the same time, the absorption spectra of the perovskite films casting from different concentrations were also measured (see Fig. S6 in the ESM). It is well known that charge transfer capability is related to charge transfer resistance, which was estimated by EIS as shown in Fig. 4(b). The equivalent circuit is placed in the inset of Fig. 4(b). The $R_{\mathrm{SH}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{CT}}$ are the sheet and charge transfer resistances, respectively [36–38]. As can be seen from Fig. 4(b), the high frequency area represents $R_{\mathrm{SH}}$ . Both devices possessed the values provided from where they were fabricated under the same condition. $R_{\mathrm{CT}}$ is associated with the low frequency area, and has a value of 1.69 and $1.98\mathrm{k}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for doped and pristine device, respectively. Consequently, the doped device shows better charge transport.
In order to fully understand the carrier transport characteristics, we measured the dark $J{-}V$ curves of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}{:}\mathrm{In}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ based hole-only and electron-only devices. Figure 4(c) shows the electron-only device with a structure of $\mathrm{FTO/TiO_{2}/C s P b I_{3}}$ (CsPbI3:InI3)/[6,6]-phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM)/Ag. The linear increase of current with voltage can be divided into three parts. Firstly, the current density increases slowly with voltage in the ohmic current region. Secondly, the trend of rapid current rise happens in the trap-fill limited (TFL) region. The coordinate value of the horizontal axis at the kink point between ohmic current region and TFL region is called trap-filled limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ . The third part is space charge limited current. The trap state density $(n_{\mathrm{trap}})$ and carrier mobility $\mu$ could be calculated by fitting the $J{-}V$ curves [35, 39, 40]. We can calculate the carrier mobility using the Mott-Gurney equation
$$
\mu=\frac{8J L^{3}}{9\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}V^{2}}
$$
where $L$ is the thickness of inorganic perovskite film, ε represents the dielectric constant under vacuum condition, and $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{0}}$ is the dielectric constant of the film. The electron mobility of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ and CsPbI3:InI3 was extrapolated to be 0.51 and $1.30\mathrm{cm}^{2}/(\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{S})$ respectively. Figure 4(d) shows the hole-only device with a structure of FTO/poly(3,4-ethylene dioxythiophene) and poly(styrene sulfonate)(PEDOT:PSS)/CsPbI3 (CsPbI3:InI3)/SpiroOMeTAD/Ag. Similarly, the hole mobility of CsPbI3 and CsPbI3:InI3 are determined to be 0.29 and $0.52~\mathrm{cm}^{2}/(\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{S})$ respectively. Obviously, electron and hole mobilities in the CsPbI3 film could be improved by InI3 incorporation. This is consistent with the conclusion of EIS. The enhanced carrier mobility is attributed to the InI3 doping, which retards the perovskite crystallization by giving enough time for the growth of large grains and the passivation of film defects.
Figure 4(e) shows the open-circuit photovoltage decay (OCVD). During the test, the devices were irradiated under standard sunlight and immediately blocked, resulting in an instantaneous decay for OCVD. The electron lifetime can be calculated from the curve according to Eq. (2) [41–44]
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{n}}=\frac{-T K_{\mathrm{B}}}{e}\Bigg(\frac{\mathrm{d}V_{\mathrm{oc}}}{\mathrm{d}t}\Bigg)
$$
where $e$ represents the elementary charge, $T$ is the temperature, and $K_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann’s constant. The relationship between electron lifetime and voltage is shown in the inset of Fig. 4(e). It can be seen that the electron lifetime in the control device is longer than that in the reference device, indicating that the carrier recombination was suppressed due to InI3 doping.
The effect of $\operatorname{InI}_{3}$ doping on the trap density of states (tDOS) is further evaluated by thermal admittance spectroscopy as shown in Fig. 4(f) [45–47]. It can be seen that the tDOS in the reference device is higher than that in the control device, indicating the passivation effect of InI3 on the CsPbI3 film. The time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectra are shown in Fig. 5(a). The PL lifetime was increased from 2.41 to 6.40 ns in fast decay process (see Table S1 in the ESM), demonstrating an effective reduction in recombination losses due to InI3 doping [48].
The effect of $\operatorname{InI}_{3}$ doping on the device performance of CsPbI3:InI3 based solar cells is finally investigated. Figure 5(b) is a schematic diagram of the device structure. The best doping ratio of $\mathrm{InI}_{3}$ was determined by the device optimization based on different dopant concentrations (see Fig. S7 and Table S2 in the ESM). Figure 5(c) shows the $J{-}V$ curves of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ and CsPbI3:InI3 based devices under $100~\mathrm{\mW/cm^{2}}$ irradiation. The champion control device yielded a PCE of $17.09\%$ with an open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of $0.99~\mathrm{V},$ a short-circuit current density $\left(J_{s c}\right)$ of $22.21~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and a fill factor (FF) of 0.77. As shown in Fig. 5(d), the PCEs of both devices were counted. It can be seen that the photovoltaic performance of the control device is enhanced. Particularly, $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ is the most increased parameter (see Fig. S8 in the ESM), which is induced by the good carrier transport and reduced interface recombination. The $J{-}V$ curves under forward and reverse scan conditions for two devices are shown in Fig. 5(e). The control device showed negligible current hysteresis. We ascribed it to the $\mathrm{InI}_{3}$ doping owing to the formation of large grains and less grain boundaries in the perovskite film [49].
In order to examine the stabilities of the reference and the control devices, their PCEs were monitored for $860\mathrm{~h~}$ in the glove box (Fig. 5(f)). The control device presented better stability relative to the reference device. Figure S9 in the ESM shows the color changes of the two films in ambient at $50\%{-}60\%$ relative humidity for $^\textrm{\scriptsize7h}$ . The pristine CsPbI3 film changed from black to yellow phase within $^\textrm{\scriptsize7h}$ , however, the control CsPbI3:InI3 film retained the black phase. The poor absorption spectrum and the appearance of yellow phase from XRD also confirmed the instability of the pristine CsPbI3 film (see Figs. S10 and S11 in the ESM).
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have incorporated $\mathrm{InI}_{3}$ into CsPbI3 inorganic perovskite for photovoltaic application. InI3 doping could retard the crystallization and control the crystal growth rate of CsPbI3 film, yielding a perovskite film with large grain size and less voids. XRD measurements revealed that $\operatorname{InI}_{3}$ doping could hinder intermediate phase formation and stabilize the black phase during annealing. As for the red-shifted UV–Vis and PL peaks, smaller $R_{\mathrm{SH}}$ and larger carrier mobility of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}{:}\mathrm{In}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ enabled the solar cells present an increased $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ from 0.89 to $0.99{\mathrm{V}}$ and deliver a largely improved PCE from $14.36\%$ to $17.09\%$ . The finding in this work gives a clue that $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ doping is an effective method to increase the $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ -based inorganic solar cells.
